+## A share house exposes you to other people's values and experiences
+
 One of the great things about living in a share house — compared to living solo in an apartment or condo — is that you get exposed to all kinds of different values and experiences from the people you live with. It's one of the best reasons to spend a chapter of your life (especially when you're young) in a place like this.
 
+## 2 out of 10 residents speak Swahili
+
 So right now The Headquarters Kobe is full, all 10 rooms occupied by 10 people, and get this: 2 of them can speak Swahili, the East African language. For native Japanese speakers (lol), a 20% Swahili proficiency rate is honestly kind of surprising, right?
 
 One of them picked it up while serving in the Japan Overseas Cooperation Volunteers in Kenya and Tanzania, and the other learned it working for a venture company doing business in Africa.
 
 ![Two residents who can speak Swahili](https://theheadquarters.jp/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/S__5177425-1024x682.jpg)
 
+## Where Swahili is actually spoken
+
 By the way, Swahili is apparently the national/official language spoken mainly along Tanzania and Kenya on Africa's east coast. It's said to be "fundamentally part of the Bantu language family, one of Africa's native language groups, but formed with vocabulary mixed in from Arabic, plus Portuguese, Turkish, Indian languages, Malay, Persian, English, and more." Sounds like a real linguistic melting pot shaped by centuries of trade.
 It's mainly spoken in the area shown below (borrowed images).
 
