+## Seven of us played a board game about anger
+
 "If you want to know someone, find out what makes them angry."
 This time, seven members of The Headquarters Kobe played a board game called *Anger Management*, built around exactly that theme of "anger."
 
 ![Residents playing a board game around the dining table](https://theheadquarters.jp/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/IMG_0479-1024x768.jpg)
 
+## The real fun is the free-flowing talk, not the topic cards
+
 In *Anger Management*, you imagine that the episode written on a topic card has happened to you, then rate how angry it would make you on an 11-point scale from 0 to 10 — and try to guess each other's ratings.
 But honestly, the real fun of this game isn't the topic cards so much as the free-flowing conversation they spark. Each story starts with "So the other day, this happened..." and ends with "...and that's why I got this angry." Packed into those little episodes is a real sense of who each person is.
 
 Comments like "when I'm happy, I can forgive almost anything" or "that's less anger than disappointment" kept the conversation about "anger" flowing, and things got lively fast. Getting absorbed in the game like this let us peek — in a fun, lighthearted way — at each other's lines that shouldn't be crossed.
 
+## Next time, shared-space etiquette as the topic
+
 Next time, we're thinking of playing again but limiting the topics to house-related episodes. Everyone would open up about how they feel regarding shared-space etiquette, and in doing so, learn what matters to each other.
 For people sharing a life together, there's probably no better form of communication than that.
