+## A former resident couple came back to visit
+
 We had a guest at the house recently. It was actually a former resident couple, T and R, who moved out of HQ at the end of June (and yes, they still match outfits like the lovey-dovey pair they are lol).  
 They're both students, so to save money they had been sharing a single room at HQ, but this time they've happily moved out to rent their own place together and start living as a couple. They contributed a lot to the house, so it was sad to see them go, but of course you have to celebrate a young couple starting their new life together, right? Haha.
 
+## Residents who found happiness with HQ as a stepping stone
+
 They're not the only ones — several other women have moved out in the past to move in with a partner (one of them actually got married and still stops by to visit sometimes 😊), and there have been cases where male and female residents who lived here at the same time ended up becoming a couple after moving out. And just recently, someone who moved in passed a qualification exam and successfully changed careers. It makes me really happy to see HQ become a stepping stone toward people's happiness. As the person running this place, that's its own kind of happiness for me.
 
+## Two things share houses with weak community have in common
+
 By the way, running a share house looks simple on the surface, but I think it's actually a fairly tricky business. Among the people who apply to live at The Headquarters, I sometimes hear from applicants currently living at other nearby share houses who say things like, "Where I live now barely has any community at all..." (T, actually, was one of those people too). I've also heard of houses with frequent trouble like theft or fighting among residents (of course, that's a minority overall — there are plenty of great houses out there). Since the whole appeal of a share house is the interaction between residents, it's a real shame when that part falls short.
 
 When I talk to people living in houses like that, two things tend to come up again and again: (1) there's little to no screening or conditions for moving in — basically anyone can join, and (2) there's almost no support or involvement from management after move-in. If you let in anyone who applies just because the rent or move-in costs are cheap, inevitably some of them cause problems. So it's important to either turn those people away, or make sure everyone moving in genuinely understands and accepts what shared living involves. On top of that, without community-building touches like welcome parties or farewell parties, it's hard for people to really get close to one another.
 
+## Making the living room and kitchen places people want to be
+
 The physical setup matters too — making common spaces like the living room somewhere people actually want to hang out (some share houses don't even have a living room). At The Headquarters, for example, we lean into home cooking and keep the kitchen well stocked with tools, and we also keep the living room stocked with games and things people can enjoy together. And if you don't make an effort to listen to residents' opinions and properly handle the various issues that come up with facilities or house rules, dissatisfaction just builds up over time. Surprisingly, there are quite a few operators out there who don't do any of this.
 
+## Running for yield vs. running for community
+
 Broadly speaking, running a share house falls under the real estate business, so for most owners and operators, "yield" tends to be the top priority. And to boost yield, the easiest path is to let anyone move in. Places that started running a share house just because it seemed like a trend, coming from other property types like apartments or condos, tend to underestimate the importance of community — and I think that's usually why things don't go so well for them.
 
 This got long, but the point was simply this: at The Headquarters Kobe, we care more about the software — the community — than the hardware. Nothing would make us happier than seeing our residents live here happily and find their own happiness along the way 😊
+
